<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isma'il Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ismail the Magnificent, Khedive Ismail</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isma'il Pasha was a grandson of Muhammad Ali who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 1863 and received the title khedive from the Ottoman sultan in 1867. He modernised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, expanded railways and education, opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> in 1869 and extended Egyptian rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. His huge debts led to European financial control, and he was deposed in 1879.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha.jpg" alt="Isma'il Pasha" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isma%27il_Pasha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Isma'il was the second of the three sons of Ibrahim Pasha and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali Pasha</a>, a family of Albanian descent. He was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>; his mother, Hoshiyar Qadin, was Circassian. He received a European education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he attended the École d'état-major. After the death of his elder brother he became heir to his uncle Sa'id, the wali of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, who sent him on missions abroad, to the Pope, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> and to the Ottoman sultan. In 1861 he led an army of 18,000 to put down an insurrection in Sudan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Isma'il was proclaimed khedive on 19 January 1863, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the other powers at first recognised him only as wali. In 1867 Sultan Abdülaziz granted a firman recognising the title of khedive in exchange for a larger tribute, in return for Egyptian help in the Cretan revolt. Another firman made the succession pass from father to son, and a decree of 1873 confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s virtual independence. He also replaced Ottoman Turkish with Arabic as the language of administration and in November 1866 opened an advisory assembly of delegates.</p><p>His spending built over 900 miles of railways, 5,000 miles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> lines, 400 bridges, harbour works at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and 4,500 schools, and a national library was founded in 1871. He annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> in 1874, but his army was beaten by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv">Yohannes IV of Ethiopia</a> at Gundet on 16 November 1875 and again at Gura in March 1876. The costs, and the lavish festival for the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> deeply in debt to European creditors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1875 Isma'il sold the Egyptian shares in the Suez Canal Company to the British government for £3,976,582, and direct intervention by the great powers followed. The Caisse de la Dette was set up in 1876, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> took joint control of much of the government's finances. In June 1879 they pressed Sultan Abdülhamid II to depose him, which was done on 26 June, and his eldest son Tewfik Pasha succeeded him.</p><p>Isma'il went into exile near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and from 1885 lived in his palace at Emirgan on the Bosporus, more or less a state prisoner, until his death on 2 March 1895. He was later buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. The historian Eugene Rogan observed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> had begun its development schemes to secure independence, yet with each new concession made itself more open to European encroachment. The city of Ismailia is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isma'il Pasha of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Isma’il Pasha</b>, who ruled from 1863, schools, a national library and new journals multiplied, and al-Ahram began publication in Alexandria in 1876.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 1860s he had proposed to <b>Isma'il Pasha</b>, Khedive of Egypt, a huge lighthouse in the form of a robed peasant woman holding a torch at the entrance to the Suez Canal; the khedive and Ferdinand de Lesseps declined it because of the cost.</blockquote><a href="/work/statue-of-liberty">Statue of Liberty · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The first, known as the &quot;Khedive's Special Salute&quot; or &quot;Salam Affandina&quot;, dated from the reign of <b>Khedive Ismail</b>: it was played at the opening of the Suez Canal and adopted in 1871, an instrumental piece without official words whose most likely author was Giuseppe Pugioli, an Italian cornetist…</blockquote><a href="/songs/biladi-biladi-biladi">Biladi, Biladi, Biladi</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1867 the Ottoman sultan granted <b>Ismail Pasha</b> the title of khedive, turning the province into the Khedivate of Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/egypt-under-muhammad-ali">Egypt under Muhammad Ali</a></li><li><blockquote>The province was much reduced over time, and in 1865 the Ottomans transferred Massawa and Suakin to <b>Khedive Ismail's</b> Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/habesh-eyalet">Habesh Eyalet</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1867 the Ottoman sultan granted <b>Ismail Pasha</b>, grandson of Muhammad Ali, the title of khedive and greater autonomy, transforming Egypt under Muhammad Ali into the Khedivate.</blockquote><a href="/state/khedivate-of-egypt">Khedivate of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1872" aria-label="Cairo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5361 1067 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Isma'il Pasha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5811 1337h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1872">What was here in 1872? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
